The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
// In ptype_seq_show() (original)
if (v == SEQ_START_TOKEN)
    seq_puts(seq, "Type Device      Function\n");
else if ((!pt->af_packet_net || net_eq(pt->af_packet_net, seq_file_net(seq))) &&
         (!pt->dev || net_eq(dev_net(pt->dev), seq_file_net(seq)))) {
    ...
    seq_printf(seq, " %-8s %ps\n",
               pt->dev ? pt->dev->name : "", pt->func);
}

// In ptype_seq_next() (original)
nxt = pt->list.next;
if (pt->dev) {
    if (nxt != &pt->dev->ptype_all)
        goto found;
    dev = pt->dev;
    ...
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because `ptype_seq_show()` reads `pt->dev` without any RCU synchronization (e.g., `rcu_dereference`), while concurrent writers can clear `pt->dev` (e.g., during device unbinding) without an RCU grace period. This violates RCU protection rules and can lead to an RCU stall or use-after-free. Additionally, `ptype_seq_next()` reads `pt->list.next` without `READ_ONCE()`, causing data races when the list is modified concurrently.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// New struct added
+struct ptype_iter_state {
+	struct seq_net_private	p;
+	struct net_device	*dev;
+};

// In ptype_get_idx()
+		if (i == pos) {
+			iter->dev = dev;
			return pt;
+		}

// In ptype_seq_show()
+	dev = iter->dev;
+	if ((!pt->af_packet_net || net_eq(pt->af_packet_net, seq_file_net(seq))) &&
+		 (!dev || net_eq(dev_net(dev), seq_file_net(seq)))) {
		...
		seq_printf(seq, " %-8s %ps\n",
+			   dev ? dev->name : "", pt->func);
	}

// In ptype_seq_next()
+	nxt = READ_ONCE(pt->list.next);
+	dev = iter->dev;
+	if (dev) {
+		if (nxt != &dev->ptype_all)
			goto found;
-		dev = pt->dev;
		...
+		nxt = READ_ONCE(dev->ptype_all.next);
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a `struct ptype_iter_state` that stores a stable `dev` pointer when a packet_type is located inside a device's list. This cached pointer is used in `ptype_seq_show()` instead of directly accessing `pt->dev`, eliminating the race because the device pointer is captured under RCU protection (the traversal uses `list_for_each_entry_rcu` and `for_each_netdev_rcu`). In `ptype_seq_next()`, `READ_ONCE()` is used for all `list.next` reads to prevent data races. The `iter->dev` is updated appropriately when moving between device lists, ensuring that the device pointer remains valid during the sequence iteration.

